62
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
So this week on the Scoutmaster Podcast, we've got a couple of things.

63
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,500
First of all, in Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less,

64
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:25,500
we're going to discuss why smart people are often bad teachers.

65
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:30,500
If you're a bad teacher, that means you're a smart person

66
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:32,000
Who knows?

67
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
But we'll talk about that more in a minute.

68
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,500
And then I've got a reply to an email about Maripatches.

69
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:38,500
So let's get started, shall we?

70
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:43,000
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

71
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
So one of my contributors, who you hear on the Scoutmaster panel discussion, Walter Underwood,

72
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,500
sent me a link to an article by Burke Camber, who is an engineer

73
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:02,000
And he has a blog,

74
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
And this article is titled: Effective Teaching Is a Long Con.

75
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
The central thought here is that smart people are bad teachers.

76
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,500
So how does this apply to Scoutmasters?

77
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:19,500
Well, you know, if you're reasonably experienced and you're skilled,

78
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:24,000
you're going to sit down and instruct scouts in a scout skill

79
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
or you're training and mentoring your youth leadership.

80
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,500
We have to accept something:

81
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:36,500
Being smart and experienced and skillful does not make you a good instructor.

82
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:39,000
It certainly helps if you are,

83
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,500
But you can't get by on being a good instructor or a mentor

84
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:45,500
just on being smart and experienced alone,

85
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:47,500
Camber said in his article. he says

86
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:51,000
smart people like to tell the whole story and end up reinventing the universe

87
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:56,500
when all they set out to do was to teach a lesson on a single topic.

88
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:00,500
Telling the whole story is kind of frustrating to the people you're trying to teach.

89
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:03,500
It's too much to take in all at once.

90
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:07,000
The smart person tends to forget that they learn the whole story

91
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,500
over years of intense learning and experience.

92
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:15,000
It's hard for the smart person to let go of pieces of the story,

93
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
to consciously admit them, even if they're not of immediate importance.

94
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:24,500
To me, that's a very fascinating and useful insight for us in our work as scout leaders.

95
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:29,000
My temptation when I am instructing a skill or I'm mentoring youth leaders,

96
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
is to be very verbose and to tell them everything,

97
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,500
To tell them absolutely everything that's involved in the subject.

98
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:38,500
So you know when this happens, right,

99
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:42,500
You're instructing or you're mentoring or talking to scouts

100
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:46,500
and you look at them and you see this kind of either a bored look

101
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:51,500
or a distracted look or a look that says

102
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:56,000
I am really hoping he's going to be finished very soon,

103
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,500
Trying to escape, you know,

104
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:02,000
And as we go through the fourth digression from the lesson or message

105
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:08,500
that we're trying to communicate. we know exactly what's going on, right?

106
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:14,000
So a good illustration of this: you're going to teach a scout how to tie a bowling. alright,

107
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Don't start with where the rope comes from

108
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,500
and what kind of plant it's made out of.

109
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:21,500
Teach the boy a bowling. alright.

110
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:24,500
If you're mentoring youth leaders, you don't need to talk to them

111
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:28,500
about every aspect of your experience and leadership

112
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:33,500
and add in 17 different kinds of anecdotes and illustrations.

113
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:37,000
You need to get to them a succinct message

114
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:44,500
about the particular skill or the particular thing that they're working at. right. then

115
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:49,000
You know, we don't have to give them the whole world and the whole story all at once,

116
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,500
but we can kind of indicate that there's a bigger world out there,

117
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:56,500
And Campbell calls this leaving a little trail of breadcrumbs. okay,

118
00:07:56,500 --> 00:08:01,500
Walter suggested this and we'll go back to the illustration of tying knots.

119
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:04,500
So you're teaching a scout to use a square knot

120
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,500
and you might drop a little breadcrumb like this:

121
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:09,500
The square knot. you know that's useful for joining two ropes.

122
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:13,500
Rock climbers use other knots that are stronger and easier on tie,

123
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:17,000
but we're just going to concentrate on the square knot right now.

124
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,500
You know that little breadcrumb there.

125
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:24,500
Yeah, you know. so you're talking to your senior patrol leader

126
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:27,500
and you're saying: well, the patrol leader's council needs to have a good agenda

127
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:32,500
and I would like to see what you come up with for that agenda.

128
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:35,500
There's a lot of places where you can learn how agendas are made

129
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:39,500
and things like that. but concentrate on what you're going to do

130
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:42,500
for the next few meetings and the next couple of addings

131
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:44,500
and see what kind of an agenda you come up with.

132
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:47,500
In other words, I've dropped a little breadcrumb there.

133
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:51,500
There's a possibility that I might learn about how to do this in another place,

134
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:57,500
or there is another level of complexity or interest or knowledge

135
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:00,500
that I can obtain about a given subject.

136
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:05,500
What's great about scoutmasters and adults volunteering and scouting

137
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:08,500
is the intensity with which they approach the idea of instructing

138
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:11,500
and helping and mentoring youth.

139
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:13,500
I think that intensity is great.

140
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:16,500
What the problem is with that intensity

141
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:20,500
is a lot of times it gets expressed in a way where it's inaccessible.

142
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:23,500
Mike Lotties wrote in to the blog on another post this week

143
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:28,500
and he mentioned a Baden Powell quote that I think is particularly applicable right here.

144
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:32,500
If a man cannot make his point to keen boys in 10 minutes,

145
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:36,500
he ought to be shot.

146
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:39,500
So when instructing and mentoring, we want to focus,

147
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:42,500
We want to get on to the most useful information

148
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:45,500
and we don't have to show the scouts the entire world

149
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:47,500
every time we speak to them.

150
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:50,500
I think we're going to find out that if we think about this

151
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:52,500
it'll make us better instructors and mentors

152
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:56,500
and it will make us more effective in our work as scout leaders.

153
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:16,500
You're my favorite all-time boy scout.

154
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:20,500
You're my favorite all-time boy scout.

155
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:24,500
In 1928,, 19-year-old Paul Cyple,

156
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,500
an eagle scout from Erie, Pennsylvania,

157
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:29,500
was picked in a nationwide contest

158
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:32,500
to accompany the first bird expedition to Antarctica.

159
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:35,500
The story of his life could almost be written

160
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:38,500
in terms of the merit badges he earned

161
00:10:38,500 --> 00:10:42,500
when he was 14,, 15, and 16 years old.

162
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:44,500
Dr Cyple has continued to play an active part

163
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:46,500
in the scout movement.

164
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:49,500
I wish every boy in America could be a scout

165
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:52,500
and I'm convinced that every scout

166
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:54,500
who takes his rank requirements

167
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,500
and his merit badges seriously

168
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:59,500
will find, as I have, that such preparedness

169
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:02,500
is the key to many doors.

170
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:05,500
You're my favorite all-time boy scout.

171
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:09,500
He's my favorite all-time boy scout.

172
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:13,500
Find me a letter, send it by name

173
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:17,500
Email. that is folks.

174
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:20,500
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

175
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:31,500
I had an interesting email discussion a week or so ago

176
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:33,500
with Frank Maynard.

177
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:35,500
He got this question

178
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:37,500
and we discussed it back and forth a little bit

179
00:11:37,500 --> 00:11:39,500
so we're going to talk about it.

180
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:41,500
This comes from a scout master,

181
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:43,500
He says. I recently discussed troop meetings

182
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:45,500
with a scout master from another troop

183
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:47,500
and I found it very interesting

184
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:49,500
because they have quite a different approach

185
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:51,500
to troop meetings.

186
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:53,500
In their troop the scouts meet for the first half

187
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:55,500
of the meeting and conduct patrol business

188
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:57,500
and demonstrations and the like.

189
00:11:57,500 --> 00:11:59,500
The second half of the meeting

190
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:01,500
the boys attend merit badge workshops

191
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:03,500
and they have four offered each quarter

192
00:12:03,500 --> 00:12:05,500
and they rotate between the merit badges.

193
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:07,500
The boys attend merit badges

194
00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:09,500
they are working on for the entire

195
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:11,500
two or three month periods.

196
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:13,500
Required merit badges are covered here.

197
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:15,500
This method's quite different from others

198
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:17,500
where I've read that merit badge instruction

199
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:19,500
is discouraged at troop meetings.

200
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:21,500
I wonder what you take out of this

201
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:23,500
and if you think it's a workable format

202
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:25,500
for troop meetings.

203
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:27,500
Frank and I went back and forth about an answer

204
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:29,500
and we both agreed: no, this really isn't

205
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:31,500
a workable format for troop meetings.

206
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:33,500
I want you to get this message.

207
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:35,500
Not that you're not allowed to do merit badges

208
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:37,500
in troop meetings, because I guess there would

209
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:39,500
be a way where I could understand

210
00:12:39,500 --> 00:12:41,500
that that might be an interesting

211
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,500
thing to do.

212
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:45,500
If the patrol leaders council decided

213
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:47,500
that they wanted all to pursue

214
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:49,500
a particular merit badge and they set it up

215
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:51,500
and they got a merit badge

216
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:53,500
counselor

217
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:55,500
and they worked it out that way.

218
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:57,500
I can understand

219
00:12:57,500 --> 00:12:59,500
the way that that would work. but setting this up

220
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:01,500
just as kind of like this is the way

221
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:03,500
we do business. first of all, we're going to have a meeting

222
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:05,500
and we're going to have these merit badge

223
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:07,500
sessions because we need to work on merit badges.

224
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:09,500
Well, that's not really why we're doing merit badges.

225
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:11,500
This is the important

226
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:13,500
message here. Don't look at this

227
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:15,500
as just like a set policy

228
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:17,500
where now you're not allowed to do merit badges

229
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:19,500
in meetings. I heard Clark say that on the podcast

230
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:21,500
and that must be a national policy

231
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:23,500
or something like that. Don't think about it that way.

232
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:25,500
Why do we have merit badges

233
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:27,500
in the first place?

234
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:29,500
Why do we have them?

235
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:31,500
Well, they definitely

236
00:13:31,500 --> 00:13:33,500
communicate useful

237
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:35,500
skills. Why has

238
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:37,500
scouting decided that merit badges are done

239
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:39,500
this way, with counselors and

240
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:41,500
stuff like that? It seems

241
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:43,500
kind of inefficient, doesn't it? I mean, the

242
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:45,500
troop meeting approach that's described here

243
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:47,500
is much more efficient.

244
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:49,500
Boys will get more merit badges

245
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:51,500
and they'll learn more skills.

246
00:13:51,500 --> 00:13:53,500
But what we're missing

